From e028e5a9ef7524550638c257bb48bd8f216fa042 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dave <dhuntlinux@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 15:56:12 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] Open what was clicked in the search results, and stop
 searching

Clicking a folder in the search results did nothing at all on a network share:
the view stayed on the results, the folder never opened, and the crawl carried
on hammering the share. Two separate causes.

thunar_standard_view_selection_changed() returned early for the whole duration
of a search, one of the "row-related callbacks" disabled to speed searching up
in 1644eb576. That dropped the selection the *user* makes by clicking a result
just as readily as the churn it was aimed at, so the "selected-files"
notification never reached the action manager. Activating then took the
"nothing is selected" path in thunar_action_manager_set_selected_files(), which
substitutes the current directory -- so Thunar dutifully re-opened the folder
being searched, which looks exactly like nothing happening. The three remaining
guards (rows-reordered, row-changed, select-after-row-deleted) still cover the
per-row work that commit was really about; measured against a 1.4 TiB SMB
share, dropping this one costs nothing: 16 results at 5s, 31 at 15s and 106 at
45s, before and after, for the same SMB daemon CPU.

Second, the crawl kept running after the activation. Poking the file goes to
the same location the search is reading, and a gvfs mount is served by one
backend process handling requests in turn, so the open queued up behind
everything the search still had outstanding. Stop the search from
thunar_action_manager_activate_selected_files() before poking -- the user has
found what they were looking for -- keeping the results already on screen.
thunar_list_model_stop_search() cancels the crawl, flushes what it had found
and emits "search-done", so the view leaves search mode exactly as it does when
a search ends by itself.

With both: a single click on a result opens that folder within four seconds,
and the share goes quiet immediately afterwards.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
